 # Default mode keeps the worktree (exit 3, nothing changed) when it has uncommitted or untracked
 # non-ignored files (its .tmp/ is excluded), or when BRANCH is not an ancestor of INTEGRATION_BRANCH.
 # Otherwise it stops every process belonging to WORKTREE (scripts/stop-run-processes: cwd,
-# executable, arguments or HOME/TMPDIR under it, plus their children; TERM, then KILL), runs
+# executable, exact HOME/TMPDIR or orphan argument paths under it; TERM, then KILL), runs
 # `git worktree remove` (never --force) and `git branch -d` (never -D). --keep-branch skips the
 # branch delete (a task other tasks may still stack on). --discard is for a cancelled task whose
 # work is abandoned by design: it skips both guards and removes with --force / -D.
 # --sweep runs the default mode on every worktree under ROOT, then `git branch -d` on each listed
 # BRANCH that still exists without a worktree.
 # --tmp DIR names the task's temp root (or, with --sweep, the run's), which must be /tmp/sp-<hex>[/<name>]:
-# once the worktree is gone (--sweep: once nothing was kept) its processes are stopped and DIR is deleted.
+# worktree and temp processes are verified stopped before any resource is removed. Survivors,
+# unknown inspection or interactive preservation retain all resources checked in that cleanup.
 #
 # Output, one fact per line: `stopped:` / `survived: <pid> <command> — <why>`, `removed: <worktree>`,
 # `deleted: <branch>`, `kept: <worktree-or-branch> — <reason>`.
-# Exit 0 done, 2 bad input, 3 something was kept.
+# Exit 0 verified done, 2 bad input, 3 something was kept, 4 process inspection unverified.
